From 9316aaaa5f707b3553c6de19fc26497e3b4813cb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: idealinsane <idealinsane@localhost>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 20:47:39 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] executor: size the coverage output buffer from the remote
 cover area

A request that collects remote coverage can carry up to kExtraCoverSize entries
in the extra record alone - 8 MiB of PCs on a 64-bit host - on top of the
per-call coverage records. The extra record is appended last, so a fixed output
cap below that combined total truncates exactly the remote coverage, and the
run silently reports fewer .extra files than executions.

On an NFS async COPY corpus, where one remote generation aggregates ~530k
entries (4.2 MiB) next to ~2.2 MiB of per-call coverage, the historical 6 MiB
kMaxOutputCoverage cut the extra record for 8 of 10 executions while the kernel
had published every entry and the executor had read all of them. The 6 MiB
constant is structurally wrong for a configuration whose remote area is 8 MiB by
construction, so derive the cap from that area size plus a documented headroom
for the per-call records instead of hardcoding it.

flatrpc.ConstMaxOutputSize, the shared-memory region the manager maps, must stay
at or above the derived value: asking for more than the manager provides makes
the executor's own coverage probe fail with errno 998 ("coverage is not
supported"). The derived value is 10 MiB against the 14 MiB currently provided.

Signed-off-by: idealinsane <idealinsane@localhost>
diff --git a/executor/executor.cc b/executor/executor.cc
index a2cf2b27e..9a6895f01 100644
--- a/executor/executor.cc
+++ b/executor/executor.cc
@@ -121,7 +121,6 @@ static void SnapshotPrepareParent();
 // Allocating (and forking) virtual memory for each executed process is expensive, so we only mmap
 // the amount we might possibly need for the specific received prog.
 const int kMaxOutputComparisons = 14 << 20; // executions with comparsions enabled are usually < 1% of all executions
-const int kMaxOutputCoverage = 6 << 20; // coverage is needed in ~ up to 1/3 of all executions (depending on corpus rotation)
 const int kMaxOutputSignal = 4 << 20;
 const int kMinOutput = 256 << 10; // if we don't need to send signal, the output is rather short.
 const int kInitialOutput = kMinOutput; // the minimal size to be allocated in the parent process
@@ -920,6 +919,23 @@ void reply_execute(uint32 status)
 void realloc_output_data()
 {
 #if SYZ_EXECUTOR_USES_FORK_SERVER
+	/*
+	 * A request that collects remote coverage can carry up to kExtraCoverSize
+	 * entries in the extra record alone - 8 MiB of PCs on a 64-bit host - on
+	 * top of the per-call coverage.  The extra record is appended last, so any
+	 * output cap below that combined total truncates exactly the remote
+	 * coverage and the run silently reports fewer .extra files than
+	 * executions; on an NFS async COPY corpus (aggregate ~530k entries) a
+	 * fixed 6 MiB cap left 2 of 10 executions with a file.  Derive the cap
+	 * from the remote area size plus a documented headroom for the per-call
+	 * records instead of hardcoding it.  flatrpc.ConstMaxOutputSize (the
+	 * shared-memory region the manager maps, currently 14 MiB) must stay at
+	 * or above the result, otherwise the executor's own coverage probe fails.
+	 */
+	const int kRemoteCoverCallHeadroom = 256 << 10;  /* entries, ~2 MiB of PCs */
+	const int kMaxOutputCoverage =
+		(kExtraCoverSize + kRemoteCoverCallHeadroom) * (int)sizeof(uint64_t);
+
 	if (flag_comparisons)
 		mmap_output(kMaxOutputComparisons);
 	else if (flag_collect_cover)
